.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OPENSSL-SKEYUTL 1ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openssl-skeyutl
.Nd opaque symmetric keys routines
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm openssl
.Ar skeyutl
.Oo
.Fl help ]
.Oo
.Fl cipher
.Em cipher ]
.Oo
.Fl skeymgmt
.Em skeymgmt ]
.Oo
.Fl skeyopt
.Em opt Ns : Ns Em value ]
.Oo
.Fl genkey ]
.Oo
.Fl provider
.Em name ]
.Oo
.Fl provider-path
.Em path ]
.Oo
.Fl provparam
.Em [name:]key=value ]
.Oo
.Fl propquery
.Em propq ]
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Not all cipher implementations support keys as raw bytes.
E.g.
PKCS#11 tokens can store them internally without any option to get the
raw byte representation.
.Pp
This tool is designed for managing opaque symmetric keys.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -cipher Em cipher
The cipher to generate the key for.
.It Sy -skeymgmt Em skeymgmt
Some providers may support opaque symmetric keys objects.
To use them, we need to know the
.Em skeymgmt .
If not specified, the name of the cipher will be used.
.Pp
To find out the name of the suitable symmetric key management, please
refer to the output of the
.Qo Li openssl list -skey-managers Qc
command.
.It Sy -skeyopt Em opt Ns : Ns Em value
To obtain an existing opaque symmetric key or to generate a new one, key
options are specified as opt:value.
These options can't be used together with any options implying raw key
either directly or indirectly.
.It Sy -genkey
Generate a new opaque key object.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl-enc 1 ,
.Xr EVP_SKEY 3
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Sy skeyutl
command was added in OpenSSL 3.5.
